    passage: `When Marta Kovalenko placed two wooden hives on the flat roof of a Kyiv office building in 2017, her colleagues assumed it was a joke. Bees, they argued, belonged in meadows, not among car parks and air-conditioning units. Seven years later the roof holds fourteen hives, and the honey they produce is sold in the café on the ground floor, usually within a week of each harvest.

Kovalenko is part of a movement that has spread to cities across Europe. Its supporters point out that urban bees often do surprisingly well. Parks, gardens, balconies and the trees lining avenues offer a wider variety of flowers than many farming areas, where a single crop may stretch to the horizon. As a result, a city hive can produce more honey than a rural one of the same size.

Yet not everyone is delighted. A number of ecologists warn that the enthusiasm for rooftop hives may be doing more harm than good. Honeybees are, after all, domesticated animals, kept in colonies of tens of thousands of insects. They compete for nectar and pollen with hundreds of species of wild bees, most of which live alone, nest in the ground or in hollow stems, and cannot travel far for food. Where hives multiply quickly, some researchers argue, wild bees may be pushed out of the best feeding spots.

"Putting a hive on a roof feels like an act of conservation," says ecologist Dmytro Levchenko, "but honeybees are not in danger. It is like trying to protect wild birds by keeping more chickens." In his view, a city that genuinely wants to help pollinators should plant more native flowers, leave patches of grass uncut and stop tidying away dead wood, which many solitary bees use as nesting sites.

Kovalenko accepts part of the criticism. Since 2021 she has refused to add new hives, and the company now spends part of its honey income on planting wildflower strips along nearby roads. "Bees taught me to look at the city differently," she says. "I notice every lime tree now. But I also learned that the most useful thing for nature is not always the most photogenic one."`,
