    id: 'starter-r6', type: 'reading', level: 'B', minutes: 30, title: "Why Teenagers Can't Get Up",
    intro: 'Read the article. Questions 1–5: choose the best answer. Questions 6–10: True, False or Not Given. Questions 11–12: complete each sentence with ONE word from the text.',
    passage: `Every weekday morning, millions of parents perform the same ritual: knocking on a bedroom door, then knocking again, then giving up and pulling back the curtains. The figure under the duvet is usually accused of laziness. Sleep scientists, however, increasingly argue that the accusation is unfair.

The explanation lies in the body clock. Around the start of puberty, the brain begins to release melatonin, the hormone that makes us feel sleepy, later in the evening than it did in childhood. A twelve-year-old who used to fade at nine o'clock may now feel wide awake until eleven or later. Because school still begins at the same hour, the teenager simply loses sleep, night after night. Experts recommend eight to ten hours for this age group; many get closer to six or seven.

The consequences go well beyond yawning in the first lesson. Researchers have linked chronic lack of sleep in adolescents to poorer memory, lower mood and slower reactions, the last a serious matter for young drivers. Weekend lie-ins, the favourite remedy, help less than they seem to: sleeping until noon on Saturday pushes the body clock even later and makes Monday morning harder still.

Some schools have responded by moving the start of the day. Where lessons begin an hour later, students tend to sleep longer rather than simply staying up later, and teachers often report that classes are more alert. The change is not easy to make. Bus timetables, parents' working hours and after-school sports all depend on the existing schedule, and critics warn that a later finish leaves less daylight for training or part-time jobs.

Meanwhile, there is plenty that families can do. Screens deserve their bad reputation, though not only because of their light: a gripping game or an endless chat keeps the mind busy long after the device has been switched off. Regular times for going to bed and getting up, even at weekends, matter more than any single trick. And perhaps parents can knock a little more gently. The teenager behind the door is not choosing to be tired; for the moment, biology is setting the alarm.`,
