    id: 'starter-r7', type: 'reading', level: 'B', minutes: 30, title: 'Treasures from the Ice',
    intro: 'Read the article. Questions 1–5: choose the best answer. Questions 6–9: True, False or Not Given. Questions 10–12: complete each sentence with ONE word from the text.',
    passage: `For thousands of years, the high mountain ice of Scandinavia and the Alps acted as a giant freezer. Objects dropped by hunters and travellers were swallowed by snow, sealed in ice and protected from the bacteria that destroy wood, leather and textiles at lower altitudes. Now, as summers grow warmer, that freezer is defrosting, and its contents are appearing at an unprecedented rate.

The most famous find remains the "Iceman", the body of a man who died in the Alps more than five thousand years ago and was discovered by two hikers in 1991. But most discoveries are humbler: arrows still attached to their feathers, a woollen tunic, wooden skis, the leather shoe of a horse. For archaeologists, such ordinary objects are precious precisely because they almost never survive elsewhere.

The difficulty is timing. Once an object emerges from the ice, the clock starts ticking. Exposed to sun, wind and rain, a wooden arrow may crack and rot within a year or two. Archaeologists therefore face a strange race: they must find artefacts after the ice has released them but before the weather destroys them. Since high mountain areas are vast and the summer season is short, teams increasingly rely on walkers, who are asked to photograph anything unusual, note its position and leave it where it is.

That last instruction is important. "People mean well when they pick something up," explains archaeologist Ingrid Solberg, "but the moment an object is moved, we lose information about where exactly it lay and what was next to it. Sometimes that context tells us more than the object itself."

There is an uncomfortable irony in all this. The same rising temperatures that are producing the finds are also making the work urgent; some ice patches that yielded hundreds of objects twenty years ago have now disappeared completely. Solberg admits that she feels a mixture of excitement and grief each season. "Every discovery is a gift," she says, "but it is a gift we are receiving because something is being lost."`,
