    id: 'starter-r8', type: 'reading', level: 'B', minutes: 25, title: 'The Last Move',
    intro: 'Read the story. Questions 1–6: choose the best answer. Questions 7–10: True, False or Not Given. Questions 11–12: complete each sentence with ONE word from the text.',
    passage: `Nobody at the club expected Oksana to reach the final, least of all Oksana. She was fifteen, had been playing seriously for barely two years, and had entered the town championship mainly because her coach had paid the fee before she could argue.

Her opponent, Mr Hrytsenko, had won the title eleven times. He was a retired engineer with a neat grey beard and a habit of humming while he thought, which other players found maddening and he claimed not to notice. He shook her hand warmly, wished her luck and then, for the next three hours, gave her none.

By the fortieth move Oksana was a pawn down and running out of time. She could feel the small crowd behind the rope losing interest; somebody's phone buzzed, a chair scraped. Hrytsenko hummed. And then, quite suddenly, he stopped.

Oksana looked at the board, then at him. He was frowning at the corner where her knight stood, a piece she had moved there almost in despair ten minutes earlier. She looked again and saw it: if he took her bishop, as he surely meant to, the knight would jump forward and his queen would be trapped. It was not a plan. It was an accident that had turned into a plan while she wasn't looking.

Hrytsenko thought for a long time. Then he did not take the bishop. He made a quiet, careful move on the other side of the board, and the game drifted into a draw that neither of them could escape.

Afterwards, as the arbiter wrote the result on the board, the old man leaned across. "That knight," he said. "Did you see it when you played it?"

Oksana hesitated. Every instinct told her to say yes. "No," she said. "I saw it just now, when you stopped humming."

Hrytsenko laughed so loudly that the arbiter dropped his pen. "Good," he said. "Then next year you will see it before you play it. That is the only difference between us." He stood up, buttoned his jacket and, still laughing, went to buy her a cup of tea.`,
