    id: 'starter-r9', type: 'reading', level: 'B', minutes: 30, title: 'When Languages Fall Silent',
    intro: 'Read the article. Questions 1–5: choose the best answer. Questions 6–9: True, False or Not Given. Questions 10–11: complete each sentence with ONE word from the text.',
    passage: `Linguists estimate that around seven thousand languages are spoken in the world today, and that a large proportion of them could fall silent by the end of this century. Most of these endangered languages have only a few thousand speakers, often elderly, and many have never been written down.

Languages rarely die because a community suddenly abandons them. The process is usually gradual and begins with a change in what parents believe will be useful for their children. When a dominant language promises jobs, education and respect, families start to speak it at home, sometimes encouraged by schools that once punished pupils for using their mother tongue. Within two or three generations, grandparents may find that they cannot hold a conversation with their grandchildren.

Why should this matter to anyone outside the community concerned? One answer is scientific. Each language encodes a particular way of dividing up the world: some have no separate words for "left" and "right" and describe positions using the points of the compass instead; others mark in their grammar whether the speaker saw an event personally or only heard about it. Every language that disappears takes with it evidence about what human minds are capable of.

A second answer concerns knowledge. Communities that have lived in one place for centuries often hold detailed knowledge of local plants, animals and weather, much of it stored in words and stories rather than in books. When the language goes, that knowledge frequently goes with it.

Revival is difficult but not impossible. Hebrew, which for centuries was used mainly in religious texts, became the everyday language of millions in the twentieth century. More modest successes include communities that have set up "language nests", in which small children spend their days with fluent elderly speakers. Technology helps too: dictionaries, recordings and even mobile apps allow a scattered community to keep in touch with its own words. What none of these tools can do, however, is replace the decision of parents to speak the language to their children. Without that, revival remains a museum rather than a living home.`,
